<?php

namespace Laravel\Nova\Fields;

/**
 * Nova types the filter callback's query param as `\Illuminate\Contracts\Database\Eloquent\Builder`,
 * but at runtime it can be either a concrete Eloquent `Builder` (a plain index request) or a `Relation`
 * (a relationship-index request) — `Laravel\Nova\Http\Requests\QueriesResources::newQuery()` explicitly
 * returns a `Relation` in that case and passes it straight into the callback unchanged. `Relation`
 * implements the contract but does not extend the concrete `Builder` class, so typing the closure param
 * as the concrete `Builder` (needed to call anything beyond the contract's surface) is reported as
 * `InvalidArgument`, and a closure narrowed to `Relation` alone would be unsound the other way.
 *
 * Redeclare the param through a bounded method-level template covering both concrete shapes, mirroring
 * the pattern used in `FieldElement.phpstub`: Psalm infers `TBuilder` from the closure's own param type
 * instead of checking against a fixed union, so a closure typed against either concrete class still
 * narrows correctly, while a genuinely wrong type (e.g. `stdClass`) is still rejected.
 *
 * Accepted trade-off: typing the closure against ONE concrete shape (just `Builder`, or just
 * `Relation`) type-checks even though the field may be reachable via both request kinds — nothing in
 * Nova's own signature says whether a given resource is ever browsed as a relation, so Psalm cannot
 * warn about it. A closure that only calls methods common to both (or that type-checks its own
 * `$query` parameter internally) is safe either way; one written against only `Builder`-specific or
 * only `Relation`-specific methods can still throw at runtime on the other request kind. Type against
 * the shared `Illuminate\Contracts\Database\Eloquent\Builder` contract instead if that matters for a
 * given field.
 */
trait Filterable
{
    /**
     * @template TBuilder of \Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Builder|\Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Relations\Relation
     * @param (callable(\Laravel\Nova\Http\Requests\NovaRequest, TBuilder, mixed, string):void)|null $filterableCallback
     * @return $this
     */
    public function filterable(?callable $filterableCallback = null) {}
}
